  <h2>The MSR setup</h2>
  <p>
    Three model-specific registers configure the fast path. <code class="mono">LSTAR</code> holds the kernel
    entry address; <code class="mono">STAR</code> encodes which GDT selectors to load on entry/exit;
    <code class="mono">SFMASK</code> says which RFLAGS bits to clear on entry (we clear IF, so the handler
    starts with interrupts off).
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// arch/x86_64/syscall.rs — init()</span>
wrmsr(MSR_EFER, rdmsr(MSR_EFER) | EFER_SCE);          <span class="c">// enable SYSCALL/SYSRET</span>
<span class="c">// STAR encodes the CS/SS selectors loaded by the CPU:</span>
<span class="c">//   [47:32] kernel CS (0x08) → SS = 0x10 automatically</span>
<span class="c">//   [63:48] user   CS base (0x10) → CS = +16 = 0x20, SS = +8 = 0x18</span>
let star: u64 = (0x0010 &lt;&lt; 48) | (0x0008 &lt;&lt; 32);
wrmsr(MSR_STAR, star);
wrmsr(MSR_LSTAR, syscall_entry as u64);
wrmsr(MSR_SFMASK, 0x200);                             <span class="c">// clear IF on entry</span></code></pre>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="label">The GDT-order gotcha</span>
    STAR doesn't name all four selectors — it names two, and the CPU derives the others by arithmetic
    (<code class="mono">+8</code>, <code class="mono">+16</code>). That's why the GDT puts user
    <em>data</em> at <code class="mono">0x18</code> and user <code class="mono">code</code> at
    <code class="mono">0x20</code>: the order is dictated by SYSRET's fixed offsets, not by taste. Get it
    backwards and <code class="mono">sysretq</code> loads a data descriptor as CS and faults with #GP.
  </div>

  <h2>The register frame</h2>
  <p>
    SYSCALL does a lot for us (saves RIP in RCX, RFLAGS in R11, applies SFMASK) but leaves RSP pointing at the
    <em>user</em> stack — so the stub's first job is to stash the user RSP and adopt the kernel stack. Then it
    pushes a 15-qword frame in a fixed order, so Rust can read it as a <code class="mono">#[repr(C)]</code>
    struct.
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// syscall.rs — the entry stub (abridged)</span>
"syscall_entry:",
"mov qword ptr [rip + sigrun_syscall_user_rsp], rsp",   <span class="c">// stash user RSP</span>
"mov rsp, qword ptr [rip + sigrun_syscall_kernel_rsp]", <span class="c">// adopt kernel stack</span>
"push r11",   <span class="c">// user RFLAGS (field 14)</span>
"push rcx",   <span class="c">// user RIP    (field 13)</span>
"push r15", "push r14", "push r13", "push r12", "push rbp", "push rbx",
"push r10",   <span class="c">// arg3 — Linux ABI uses r10 because SYSCALL clobbers rcx</span>
"push r9", "push r8", "push rdx", "push rsi", "push rdi",
"push rax",   <span class="c">// syscall number (lands at [rsp+0])</span>
"mov rdi, rsp",
"call rust_syscall_handler",
"add rsp, 8",                        <span class="c">// discard the old rax slot</span>
<span class="c">/* pop everything back, restore user RSP, sysretq */</span>
"sysretq",</code></pre>

  <h2>Dispatch: five calls that actually work</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>rax</th><th>Name</th><th>What it does today</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">1</td><td class="mono">SYS_WRITE</td><td>fd 1 or 2 → <code class="mono">serial::write</code> on the byte slice; returns length or −1 (EBADF)</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">39</td><td class="mono">SYS_GETPID</td><td>Returns the current <code class="mono">TaskId</code> from the scheduler</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">24</td><td class="mono">SYS_SCHED_YIELD</td><td>Calls <code class="mono">scheduler::tick()</code> to give up the CPU</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">35</td><td class="mono">SYS_NANOSLEEP</td><td>Busy-wait using <code class="mono">timer::sleep_ns</code> (not a blocking sleep yet)</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">60</td><td class="mono">SYS_EXIT</td><td>Logs, then <code class="mono">switch_to_idle()</code> — the frame is leaked, no task teardown yet</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">—</td><td>anything else</td><td>Logs <code class="mono">unknown nr=…</code> and returns <code class="mono">u64::MAX</code> (ENOSYS)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">Two ABI worlds — know this</span>
    <code class="mono">libs/syscall-api/src/number.rs</code> defines 31 <em>different</em> syscall numbers
    (<code class="mono">EXIT=0, WRITE=23, IPC_SEND=30, CAP_GRANT=40, …</code>) for a future userspace ABI, while
    the kernel's dispatch table uses the Linux x86-64 numbers above. They are not reconciled yet. If an
    interviewer notices, the right answer is: "the kernel implements a Linux-compatible subset so real
    userspace tooling could work, and the richer SIGRUN-specific ABI is designed but not yet wired — unifying
    them is a known task."
  </div>

  <h2>The ring-3 demo</h2>
  <p>
    After the kernel tasks are spawned, <code class="mono">launch_user_demo()</code> allocates a 4&nbsp;KiB user
    stack and executes <code class="mono">iretq</code> with an SS:RSP:RFLAGS:CS:RIP frame — CS =
    <code class="mono">0x23</code> (user code, DPL 3), SS = <code class="mono">0x1b</code> (user data) — which
    drops the CPU to ring 3 at <code class="mono">user_init_process</code>. That function then does real
    syscalls with inline asm: <code class="mono">sys_write</code> of a message, <code class="mono">sys_getpid</code>,
    another write, and finally <code class="mono">sys_exit</code>. It works because the U/S hack in
    <a href="/memory">memory</a> made the kernel's pages user-accessible — the demo is linked into the kernel
    binary but executes at CPL 3.
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// main.rs — the ring-3 jump (IRETQ frame is pushed high-to-low)</span>
core::arch::asm!(
    "push &#123;ss&#125;",  "push &#123;user_rsp&#125;",  "pushfq",
    "or qword ptr [rsp], 0x200",     <span class="c">// ensure IF=1 in the user's RFLAGS</span>
    "push &#123;cs&#125;",  "push &#123;user_rip&#125;", "iretq",
    ss = in(reg) 0x1bu64, cs = in(reg) 0x23u64, ...
    options(noreturn),
);</code></pre>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: syscalls</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why SYSCALL/SYSRET instead of <code class="mono">int 0x80</code>?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Speed and control. An <code class="mono">int</code> goes through the IDT (memory lookup), pushes a full
          interrupt frame, and requires an <code class="mono">iret</code> that revalidates a lot of state.
          SYSCALL jumps straight to the address in LSTAR with the CPU's own register saves, and SYSRETQ returns
          without a stack switch or descriptor reload. The trade is that the kernel must do that bookkeeping
          itself — hence the stub's explicit RSP handoff. Linux uses exactly this pair on x86-64.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "What does SYSCALL not do that you must?" — It doesn't switch stacks, doesn't validate the user's RIP/RSP are canonical, and doesn't mask all state; RSP still points into user memory, so until the stub swaps it, the kernel is running on a stack the user controls — a classic privilege-escalation surface (privilege-separation "SYSRET bug" territory) that a production kernel handles with a switch stack and strict validation.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why is argument 3 in <code class="mono">r10</code> and not <code class="mono">rcx</code>?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Because SYSCALL overwrites RCX with the user's return RIP (and R11 with RFLAGS). The hardware steals
          RCX, so the syscall ABI uses R10 as the fourth argument register instead — Linux made the same choice,
          and SIGRUN copies it rather than inventing a convention. This is the kind of detail that separates
          "I read the Intel manual" from "I copied a blog".
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>What's the security hole in <code class="mono">sys_write</code>, and how would you fix it?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          It builds a slice straight from the user's pointer with
          <code class="mono">core::slice::from_raw_parts(buf_ptr, len)</code> and no validation at all — the
          comment in the source admits it. A malicious or buggy user program could pass a kernel address, an
          unmapped address, or a huge length. The fix is a <code class="mono">copy_from_user</code> helper
          that range-checks the pointer against the current address space's user regions, faults cleanly
          instead of panicking, and copies into a kernel buffer before trusting the bytes. Enabling
          SMAP/SMEP in CR4 would also stop the kernel from touching user memory without an explicit window.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>What actually happens when the demo calls <code class="mono">sys_exit</code>?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The handler logs the code and calls <code class="mono">scheduler::switch_to_idle()</code>, which
          context-switches to the idle task using <code class="mono">BOOT_SAVED_RSP</code> as a throwaway slot —
          it never returns to the syscall frame. So the user stack, the task, and its frame are simply
          abandoned; the timer-driven scheduler then resumes the kernel tasks. There's no
          <code class="mono">exit</code> that frees resources, notifies a parent, or reaps the task — because
          there are no parents or process lifecycle yet. That's the honest gap; saying so demonstrates you know
          what a real <code class="mono">exit</code> entails (zombie state, reaping, fd/cap cleanup).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Why does it use <code class="mono">switch_to_idle</code> and not a normal return?" — A normal <code class="mono">sysretq</code> would return to user code that is supposed to be gone; the process has no valid continuation, so the kernel switches away entirely and lets the scheduler pick the next runnable task.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

